[candidate · 5:38] I'd start with COGS and store labor together — the variable store-level lines. Two reasons. First, size: in a coffee chain those two are typically the largest cost lines, so an 18-point margin swing is most plausibly driven there. A small line like marketing would have to more than triple to explain this.

Second, the revenue signal points that way. Revenue grew 15% and margin still collapsed, which means costs grew faster than the top line. Truly fixed costs like corporate overhead don't scale with revenue, so if anything they should have helped margin through operating leverage. Costs outrunning revenue growth suggests either input prices rose — green coffee and dairy are volatile commodities — or unit economics per store deteriorated.

If COGS and labor turn out flat as a percent of revenue, then I'd pivot to the fixed side, particularly rent and new-store buildout costs from expansion.
