[candidate · 13:59] Beans as a share of revenue is the product of two ratios. COGS is 42% of revenue, and beans are 25% of COGS — so beans are 25% of that 42%.

0.25 × 42% = 10.5% of revenue.

Concretely, if revenue were $100, COGS is $42, and a quarter of that $42 is $10.50 spent on beans. So beans were 10.5 cents of every revenue dollar two years ago.

The reason I want it expressed against revenue rather than against COGS is that the margin decline is measured in points of revenue. Everything has to reconcile to the same denominator, or I can't tell whether a line grew in absolute terms or just grew as a share of a COGS base that was itself expanding.

So if I get today's bean share of COGS, I'll do the same conversion — multiply it by 58% — and the difference from 10.5% is the bean contribution to the 16-point COGS gap.
