[candidate · 21:08] I was assuming zero elasticity, which isn't defensible — that was a best-case bound, not a forecast.

Let me redo it properly. Index revenue at 115 with a 6% margin, so profit is about 6.9 and costs are 108.1. Of those costs, roughly 80 points are variable — COGS at 58% and labor at 22% of revenue — and about 16 are fixed overhead.

Now raise price 10% and assume volume falls 5%, which implies elasticity of about -0.5, plausible for specialty coffee with habitual customers. Revenue becomes 115 × 1.10 × 0.95 = 120.2. Variable costs fall with volume: 80 × 0.95 = 76. Fixed stays at 16. Total cost 92, profit 28.2, margin about 23%.

So it still works, and it works largely because losing volume sheds variable cost — at 80% variable, unit contribution is thin enough that trading a little volume for price is favorable.

The number I'd actually want is a price test in a few markets to measure real elasticity rather than assume -0.5.
