[candidate · 23:59] My recommendation: raise menu prices roughly 10 to 15 percent, phased and market-tested, because this is a pricing problem, not a cost problem.

The data reconciles completely. Beans were 10.5% of revenue and rose 40%, giving 14.7. The other inputs were 31.5% of revenue and rose 37.5%, giving 43.3. Together that's 58% — exactly today's COGS. So essentially all of the 16-point COGS increase is broad input inflation, with zero offsetting price action in two years.

Labor held at 22% and volume grew 15%, so operations and demand are healthy. There's no efficiency fix that recovers 16 points.

Risks: real elasticity may exceed my -0.5 assumption, and we lack competitor pricing — if peers also held prices flat, we'd be first mover.

Next steps: price test in 15 to 20 stores, get competitor benchmarks, and hedge or renegotiate bean and dairy contracts to stop the bleeding going forward.
